1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,044
An AI generated fix can look right and still lose data.

2
00:00:04,044 --> 00:00:06,985
Before we ship, we need evidence it works.

3
00:00:06,985 --> 00:00:16,544
We built ProofPatch to make that evidence reproducible: show the original failure, test the repair against unchanged checks, and give reviewers a package they can rerun.

4
00:00:16,544 --> 00:00:17,647
Verify the fix.

5
00:00:17,647 --> 00:00:18,750
Before you ship.

6
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:21,414
Here is the problem behind our project.

7
00:00:21,414 --> 00:00:24,839
A patch can look correct while silently losing data.

8
00:00:24,839 --> 00:00:29,025
In our synthetic task export, records share the same creation time.

9
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:32,831
At a page boundary, the original cursor skips task four.

10
00:00:32,831 --> 00:00:38,920
We repair this using both timestamp and identifier, then demonstrate exactly how we verified the change.

11
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:42,205
We used IBM Bob during our original development.

12
00:00:42,205 --> 00:00:51,648
Our reusable local Bob skill structures the workflow around understanding the issue, reproducing it, preserving the tests, repairing the code and packaging evidence.

13
00:00:51,648 --> 00:00:56,164
After using our allocated credits, we continued improving and testing locally.

14
00:00:56,164 --> 00:01:00,680
This makes the verification stage repeatable whenever a reviewer needs it.

15
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:04,478
Our verification contract links the original failure to the repair.

16
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:07,896
First, we check the protected baseline, fixture and tests.

17
00:01:07,896 --> 00:01:16,252
Then we require the known assertion to fail on the original code and the identical complete suite to pass on the candidate.

18
00:01:16,252 --> 00:01:20,810
Changed tests, missing results and execution errors cannot earn a verified result.

19
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:24,104
Now let's walk through our recorded verification results.

20
00:01:24,104 --> 00:01:27,399
With the original selected, we reproduce the defect.

21
00:01:27,399 --> 00:01:33,164
Thirteen behavior checks fail, seventeen pass, and task four is missing from the preview.

22
00:01:33,164 --> 00:01:36,870
This establishes the specific failure our repair must address.

23
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:43,716
With our repaired candidate selected, all thirty behavior checks pass, with no errors or skipped checks.

24
00:01:43,716 --> 00:01:46,711
All eight task identifiers appear exactly once.

25
00:01:46,711 --> 00:01:53,557
We check page sizes two, three and four, larger exports, ordering, preservation of inputs and termination.

26
00:01:53,557 --> 00:01:56,980
The candidate earns verified against this test suite.

27
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:01,153
We also challenge our verifier with a deliberately incorrect repair.

28
00:02:01,153 --> 00:02:04,491
It repeats records and fails twenty two checks.

29
00:02:04,491 --> 00:02:05,743
ProofPatch rejects it.

30
00:02:05,743 --> 00:02:12,420
This negative control matters because a useful verification workflow must demonstrate rejection as well as success.

31
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:15,259
Our evidence package makes the result reproducible.

32
00:02:15,259 --> 00:02:22,965
It includes the exact executed source and tests, the fixture, patch, complete logs, both test reports and content checksums.

33
00:02:22,965 --> 00:02:28,643
We test that the exported package can be extracted and its candidate rerun successfully.

34
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:33,510
Reviewers receive the evidence behind the result, not just a green status.

35
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:36,239
We tested the verifier itself, too.

36
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:43,517
Thirty five system and interface checks pass, including changed tests, timeouts, concurrent runs and evidence downloads.

37
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:46,701
Our current prototype covers one bundled case.

38
00:02:46,701 --> 00:02:51,250
Verification supports a human decision within the test suite's scope.

39
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:54,503
Developers and reviewers gain a repeatable evidence handoff.

40
00:02:54,503 --> 00:03:00,602
For IBM's developer ecosystem, our project offers a reusable verification pattern around Bob assisted work.

41
00:03:00,602 --> 00:03:04,668
Governance teams can trace what was tested and rerun it.

42
00:03:04,668 --> 00:03:10,360
End users benefit indirectly when defects such as incomplete exports are caught before release.

43
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:15,274
Our first users are small Python teams already reviewing AI assisted changes.

44
00:03:15,274 --> 00:03:25,102
Tests and continuous integration are established tools; our focus is joining the original failure, unchanged checks and exact execution snapshots in one review package.

45
00:03:25,102 --> 00:03:30,426
Next, we plan repository and CI adapters and pilots that measure review effort.

46
00:03:30,426 --> 00:03:35,340
Hosted team history and CI reporting are potential revenue streams to validate.

47
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:46,737
Before approving a repair, reviewers should be able to answer three questions: did we reproduce the bug, did the repair pass the unchanged checks, and can someone else rerun the result?

48
00:03:46,737 --> 00:03:49,678
That is what we built ProofPatch to deliver.

49
00:03:49,678 --> 00:03:50,781
Verify the fix.

50
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:51,884
Before you ship.

51
00:03:51,884 --> 00:03:55,560
This video uses AI generated narration for our team's presentation.
